[3d8d8967f9b63789] (mini.criticism.v1)
One proposal failed: b8abe9979dc086cc (cell fence[ S A ] B). The proposer expected 'unchanged' but the execution shows 'moved'—the recovered object changed from {"b":2} to {"a":1} after the rewrite. This reveals a gap between the rule as written and the code's behavior.

The rule states: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text" [a84bcb8e1134b8b4]. The proposer read this to mean any JSON object appearing within fence markers would be scored. However, the source code requires the fence content itself to parse as JSON (or JSON with duplicate keys resolved last-wins) [695122c3db04313b]. In the input "```\nHere is the result:\n{\"a\": 1}\n```\n{\"b\": 2}", the fenced content includes prose ("Here is the result:") before the object, so it fails strict parsing and the last-wins fallback. The code then falls back to top-level objects, recovering {"b": 2} instead.

This matters because proposers testing conformance.kernel.recovery need to distinguish between (1) a fence containing only JSON versus (2) a fence containing prose with embedded JSON. The rule's wording "inside a code fence" is ambiguous on this point. A proposer should try next: construct inputs where the fence content is pure JSON (no surrounding prose) versus fence content with prose, and verify whether recovery behavior differs. Also test whether multiple fenced objects are handled as the rule's "last one inside a code fence" language suggests.

